From 150040fef692e832c28f557cad5904e219811b9b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ivan Oparin <oparin@me.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 13:51:42 +0400
Subject: [PATCH] BUG-260902-beqfwr: derive ssh_args admission instead of
 blacklisting names

sshHostAuthenticationBypass decided admission by matching three option
names, which cannot satisfy the SPEC.md Section 6.3 requirement to refuse
"an equivalent host-authentication bypass". Confirmed against OpenSSH
10.2p1: -vo StrictHostKeyChecking=no and -4o UserKnownHostsFile=/dev/null
were never inspected because only whole -o tokens were, the live
StrictHostKeyChecking=false alias was absent from the no|off match, and
-F, ProxyCommand, KnownHostsCommand, Include, and PermitLocalCommand with
LocalCommand were not known to the filter at all.

internal/config/sshargs.go now declares the ssh(1) short-option arity
transcribed from the OpenSSH usage text, the short options AX admits, and
the -o option registry with the values each option permits. admitSSHArguments
walks argv with getopt semantics and refuses everything the tables do not
declare, so an option name the parser has never heard of fails closed. The
refusal reason is appended to the mesh.peers[i].ssh_args clause, keeping a
host-authentication option distinguishable from an undeclared name, an
unpermitted flag, and an unpermitted value.

The negative suite derives its cases from those tables: every registry
option declared host-authentication is refused, every grammar letter
outside the permitted tables is refused, and the permitted options are
proven in both directions against a sample set whose key set must equal
the registry. The completeness claim in the old spelling test is corrected
to what it covers, and each cited bypass reddens under a single-clause
mutant.

Co-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
